#{[17592186342007 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right).$"] [17592186342010 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.$"] [17592186344992 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).$"] [17592186344993 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}.$"] [17592186344994 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}.$"] [17592186345000 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$"] [17592186345001 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\n$$"] [17592186345002 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\n$$"] [17592186345003 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=$"] [17592186345004 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186345005 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=$"] [17592186345118 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{n}^{10}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{n}^{10}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."] [17592186345119 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1)=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"] [17592186345120 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186345140 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=10+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."] [17592186345142 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{20\\operatorname{log}⁡n}{{n}^{2}-1}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{20\\operatorname{log}⁡n}{{n}^{2}-1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186345144 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186345145 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}{3{e}^{n}}+1)=1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}{3{e}^{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}{3{e}^{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}{3{e}^{n}}=1+0=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $1.$"] [17592186348055 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x}.$"] [17592186348058 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)}+6)=$"] [17592186348058 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{5}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)}=\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)}+6)=\\infty \n$$"] [17592186348059 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10x}{{e}^{x}}-2).$"] [17592186348059 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡10x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10x}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10x}{{e}^{x}}-2)=0-2=-2.\n$$"] [17592186348060 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6x)}.$"] [17592186348060 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6x)}=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186348061 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}).$"] [17592186348062 :question/problem "Given that $p(x)={x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=-7{x}^{4},$ what is $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}?$"] [17592186348614 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }({\\int }_{-2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186348768 :answer/value "\\lim_{b\\to (-3)^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186349048 :answer/value "\\lim_{b\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{b}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186349911 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186350163 :answer/value "\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186350179 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to (-8)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186350204 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to {3}^{-}}({\\int }_{-2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186350477 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186350498 :answer/value "\\lim_{b\\to (-6)^{+}}({\\int }_{b}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186350702 :answer/value "\\lim_{b\\to {3}^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)"] [17592186360892 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186360951 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=3"] [17592186360965 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=3"] [17592186361088 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186361222 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3"] [17592186361253 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3"] [17592186361316 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=0"] [17592186361344 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186361444 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186361501 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-2"] [17592186361710 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=-2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=-3"] [17592186361730 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-1"] [17592186361840 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186361872 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=3"] [17592186361907 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1"] [17592186362355 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{12}).$"] [17592186362357 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{({t}^{2}+1)}^{4}.$"] [17592186362630 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}}=$"] [17592186362928 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n}=\n$$"] [17592186362935 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-20n}=\n$$"] [17592186362948 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-n\\theta }=\n$$"] [17592186362955 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{-4n}=\n$$"] [17592186363017 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n/2}=\n$$"] [17592186363692 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363695 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n(x+y)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363698 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{3n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363941 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{9n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363944 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/5}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363947 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{7nyz}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363950 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186363953 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x+22}-4}{x+3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186364199 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-3}{x-8}.$"] [17592186364206 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{7\\sqrt{x}-x}.$"] [17592186364282 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 81}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-9}{x-81}.$"] [17592186364289 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 27}\\frac{x-27}{\\sqrt{3x}-9}.$"] [17592186364859 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}-x-1}=$"] [17592186365568 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}+9x-1}{3x+5}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186365571 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $m$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}]$"] [17592186366158 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$"] [17592186366158 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=4.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-4}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{i}=4+\\frac{3i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={x}_{i}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{7}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $b=7.$"] [17592186367247 :question/problem "Find the limit  $\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{1}{t-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{1-t}\\,k.$"] [17592186367275 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 2}||f(s)||,$ if $f(s)=⟨{e}^{s-2},\\,\\sqrt{6-s},\\,\\frac{3}{s+2}⟩.$"] [17592186367282 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 2}||f(s)||,$ if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{s}^{3}-5⟩.$"] [17592186367985 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 0}||f(s)||$, if $f(s)=⟨{e}^{s},\\frac{{s}^{2}-2}{s+6},{4}^{s}⟩.$"] [17592186367992 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨4{s}^{7}+13,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{{s}^{2}+13}{{e}^{s}}⟩.\n$$"] [17592186369553 :question/problem "Find $g(1)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(1)=⟨0,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)+g(t)]=⟨-1,4,2⟩\\,.\n$$"] [17592186369560 :question/problem "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(4)=⟨1,2,-2⟩$ and $g(4)=⟨0,2,3⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 4}[3f(t)-2g(t)].\n$$"] [17592186371000 :question/problem "Consider a vector-valued function $f(u)$ that is continuous over all real numbers and the real-valued function $u(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-t).$ Then $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(u(t))=$"] [17592186371014 :question/problem "Find $g(1)$ given that $g(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(1)=5$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 1}[u(t)g(t)]=⟨5,-10,20⟩\\,.\n$$"] [17592186371028 :question/problem "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-2)=⟨0,3,-9⟩$ and $g(-2)=⟨2,0,1⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to -2}[\\frac{f(t)}{3}+2g(t)].\n$$"] [17592186376171 :question/problem "The notation\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}=0\n$$\nmeans the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}} \n$ε>0,$\n{{field-2}} \n$N\\in N$ $,$ \n{{field-3}} \n$n\\in N,$\n{{field-4}} \n$\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|$ \n{{field-5}}"] [17592186376171 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that $\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}=0,$ means the following: \n\nFor any real $ε>0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have \n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<ε.\n$$\n\nThis can also be expressed as follows:\n\nFor any real $ε>0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n,$ if $n\\ge N,$ then \n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<ε.\n$$\n\nTranslating this into symbolic form, we get\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<ε.\n$$"] [17592186376552 :question/problem "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186377695 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$"] [17592186377708 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$"] [17592186378230 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(3)=-1$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=3$” is true, and “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=3$” and “It is continuous at $x=3$” are false."] [17592186378237 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-3)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-3$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=-1$ and $x=4,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=2$ only."] [17592186378622 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$"] [17592186378629 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$"] [17592186378636 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186379716 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(5n-3)(5n+3)}{n(2-25n)}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186379841 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-2)=3$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=3,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=3$ only."] [17592186380041 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z}=\n$$"]}
